    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1532">

                <pb facs="1243" n="1127" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg1127"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 817.48.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to the
                            Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Managua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-08-05">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 5, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Owing to a partial failure of crops last year and the financial exhaustion of the
                    Republic under the Zelaya régime, there was wide-spread want approaching famine
                    throughout the country before the outbreak of the present trouble. On account of
                    existing hostilities and consequent prospect of increase of this suffering, does
                    the Department think it advisable to ascertain whether the American Red Cross is
                    prepared to send on short notice supplies to be distributed to non-combatants
                    under American supervision?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Weitzel.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
